We want to split the cases between Balanced, Down on left, and Down on right. This can be done by finding where they are equal.
	12n^2 - 94n + 264 = 47n - 6n^2
	18n^2 - 141n + 264 = 0
	n^2 - 47n/6 + 44/3 = 0
	(n - 47/12)^2 = 2209/144 - 44/3
	(n - 47/12)^2 = 97/144

The midpoint is given by 47/12 = 48/12 - small = 4 - small. The deviation of the roots from the midpoint is 97/144 = 100/144 - small = (10/12)^2. (4 - small) + (10/12 - small) and (4 - small) - (10/12 - small) are the two solutions. The latter of these will be very close to an integer meaning when the quadratic is evaluated on the integers, this solution will minimise the difference between Balanced and Down on the left. This gives us the first weighing as three balls vs three balls. Six weighings can distinguish between just under three times as many states as necessary means there is a lot of slack. Given this, it may be possible to find a solution with five balls on each side. As a check, the number of states is given explicitly below and it appears that four balls on each side could also work.

